,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9,8],[8,8,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9,8],[8,8,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9,8],[8,8,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9,8],[8,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,8]],"output":[[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,9],[9,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,9],[9,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,9],[9,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,9],[9,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9],[9,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,9],[9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Every input has a row of small coloured glyphs along the top. Each glyph is a 3×3 or 3×4 pattern, and some of them are split into several pieces of the same colour. Lower down there is one large rectangular **frame**. Inside the frame there are a few solid **blocks** of a single fill colour, and the rest of the interior is background.\n\n**The key observation: the interior is a coarse grid of cells.**\n- Pair 3: the interior is 6×8 and every glyph's bounding box is 3×4. So the interior is a 3×4 grid of 2×2 cells.\n  - The single 3-coloured block (2×4) covers cells (2,2) and (2,3).\n  - The output interior, read at that cell scale, is `1111 / 0110 / 1111`. That is exactly the pattern of the colour-1 glyph.\n- Pair 1: the interior is 12×12 and the glyphs are 3×3, so the cells are 4×4.\n  - The blocks cover cells (0,0), (0,1) and (1,2).\n  - The output pattern is `110 / 011 / 100`, which is the colour-4 glyph.\n  - The colour-2 glyph also contains (0,0) and (0,1), but not (1,2), so it is ruled out.\n- Pair 2: the interior is 12×9 and the glyphs are 4×3, so the cells are 3×3. The same reasoning applies.\n\n**The rule.**\n1. Split the interior into cells of size (interior size ÷ glyph size).\n2. Find the cells that the given blocks occupy.\n3. Choose the unique glyph whose pattern is filled at all of those cells.\n4. Output the frame cropped out of the grid. Each interior cell takes the fill colour where the glyph is filled and the background colour where it is empty.\n\nThe blocks are only partial evidence: they show where the glyph *is* filled, not where it is empty.\n\n**The test input.**\n- The 9-frame has a 12×16 interior and the glyphs are 3×4, so the cells are 4×4.\n- The 7-blocks sit at cells (0,2) and (2,3).\n- Checking each glyph at those two cells:\n  - glyph 2: filled at (0,2), empty at (2,3) → rejected\n  - glyph 1: filled at (0,2), empty at (2,3) → rejected\n  - glyph 4: empty at (0,2) → rejected\n  - glyph 6 (`1010 / 0111 / 1001`): filled at both → the only match\n- So the output is the 14×18 frame with glyph 6 drawn in 7s at 4×4 scale.\n\n**Construction stages.** The program emits two canvases:\n1. The cropped frame with its interior cleared to background.\n2. The interior filled with the chosen glyph scaled up, which is the final output.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    frame = max(objs, key=lambda o: o[\"size\"])\n    fy0, fx0, fy1, fx1 = frame[\"box\"]\n    fcol = frame[\"colour\"]\n    inner = g[fy0+1:fy1, fx0+1:fx1]\n    H, W = inner.shape\n    fillmask = (inner != bg) & (inner != fcol)\n    fill_cols = [c for c in np.unique(inner[fillmask])]\n    fill = int(fill_cols[0])\n\n    # glyphs: non-bg cells outside the frame box, grouped by colour\n    glyphs = {}\n    for y in range(g.shape[0]):\n        for x in range(g.shape[1]):\n            if g[y, x] == bg or (fy0 <= y <= fy1 and fx0 <= x <= fx1):\n                continue\n            glyphs.setdefault(int(g[y, x]), []).append((y, x))\n    pats = []\n    for c, cells in glyphs.items():\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        p = np.zeros((y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1), bool)\n        for y, x in cells:\n            p[y - y0, x - x0] = True\n        pats.append(p)\n\n    out = g[fy0:fy1+1, fx0:fx1+1].copy()\n    out[1:-1, 1:-1] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Crop the frame and clear its interior to background.\")\n\n    chosen = None\n    for p in pats:\n        ph, pw = p.shape\n        if H % ph or W % pw:\n            continue\n        ch, cw = H // ph, W // pw\n        ok = True\n        for i in range(ph):\n            for j in range(pw):\n                if fillmask[i*ch:(i+1)*ch, j*cw:(j+1)*cw].any() and not p[i, j]:\n                    ok = False\n        if ok:\n            chosen = (p, ch, cw)\n            break\n    p, ch, cw = chosen\n    big = np.kron(p.astype(int), np.ones((ch, cw), int)).astype(bool)\n    out[1:-1, 1:-1] = np.where(big, fill, bg)\n    emit(out, \"Fill interior with the glyph consistent with the given blocks, scaled to cell size.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The key observation: the interior is a coarse grid of cells.**"}],"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grid is made of.** Every input has a row of small coloured glyphs along the top."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each glyph is a 3×3 or 3×4 pattern, and some of them are split into several pieces of the same colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the interior is 6×8 and every glyph's bounding box is 3×4."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Inside the frame there are a few solid **blocks** of a single fill colour, and the rest of the interior is background."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The key observation: the interior is a coarse grid of cells.**"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the interior is 6×8 and every glyph's bounding box is 3×4."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the interior is 6×8 and every glyph's bounding box is 3×4."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Output the frame cropped out of the grid."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The cropped frame with its interior cleared to background."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The output interior, read at that cell scale, is `1111 / 0110 / 1111`."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- So the output is the 14×18 frame with glyph 6 drawn in 7s at 4×4 scale."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The interior filled with the chosen glyph scaled up, which is the final output."}],"instructions":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grid is made of.** Every input has a row of small coloured glyphs along the top."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each glyph is a 3×3 or 3×4 pattern, and some of them are split into several pieces of the same colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the interior is 6×8 and every glyph's bounding box is 3×4."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f6dbeb95c84e580bcc90e150811021ef6e9b9fb621062177853e01dde6747f05","task_sha256":"f44865d8a3e4a9381f450f421de6755bf6c136fc99f8dcdf88db29b47600171a","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
